+\begin{remark}[Enlightened inertia]
+\label{rem:stickiness}
+Because switching is itself costly, acquiring a distinction and acting on it are separate decisions.
+An agent elects inquiry only when the achievability-weighted horizon gain, net of the switching hurdle, covers the inquiry cost (\cref{prop:participation}); but the deliberative leverage $\widehat B_i$ is an estimate, and the refinement that actually arrives may recommend a policy change whose realized horizon gain falls below $\kappa_i$.
+The agent then refines its awareness and updates its model while carrying its old policy forward: it knows the world is not what it thought, yet the change this implies is not worth its cost.
+This is a second source of persistence, distinct from an unpriced question---the model moves and behavior does not---and it feeds the performance differences of \cref{sec:dynamics}.
+Under the pointwise-transparency condition of \cref{rem:certification} the estimate is exact and the two decisions align, so enlightened inertia is a feature of opacity: the very gap the achievability weight prices.
+\end{remark}
+
